<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<topic id="1a01e248-9fbf-4065-84c2-89fce456723c" revisionNumber="1">
  <developerConceptualDocument xmlns="http://ddue.schemas.microsoft.com/authoring/2003/5" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">
    <introduction>
      <para>
      TheIntensive.Services.CTKAPIWrapper Library is a set of .NET "wrapper" classes that make it easier for .NET applications
      to communicate with CORE using the CTKAPI web endpoints.  The classes are grouped into three namespaces
      </para>
		<table>
		  <tableHeader>
		    <row>
		      <entry><para>Namespace</para></entry>
		      <entry><para>Description</para></entry>
		    </row>
		  </tableHeader>
		  <row>
		      <entry><para><codeEntityReference>N:Rackspace.CTKAPI.Net</codeEntityReference></para></entry>
		      <entry>
		      	<para>
		      		These classes provide "low-level" functionality to build a request or an action, 
		      		submit it to the CORE CTKAPI endpoint and perform some basic formatting of the returned data.
		      	</para>
		      </entry>
		  </row>
		  <row>
		      <entry><para><codeEntityReference>N:Rackspace.CTKAPI.Net.CTKObjects</codeEntityReference></para></entry>
		      <entry>
		      	<para>
		      		These are a set of "convenience" classes that make working with common objects a little easier. They are designed to mimic
		      		the CORE CTKAPI objects as much as possible.  
		        </para>
			  </entry>
		  </row>
		  <row>
		      <entry><para><codeEntityReference>N:Rackspace.CTKAPI.Net.Exceptions</codeEntityReference></para></entry>
		      <entry ><para>Contains all of the exceptions that can be thrown by any class in the Library</para></entry>
		  </row>
		</table>
		

    </introduction>
    <!-- Add one or more top-level section elements.  These are collapsible.
         If using <autoOutline />, add an address attribute to identify it
         and specify a title so that it can be jumped to with a hyperlink. -->
    <section address="Getting Started">
      <title>Getting Started</title>
      <content>
        <!-- Uncomment this to create a sub-section outline
        <autoOutline /> -->
        <list>
        	<listItem>Add reference to xxxxxxxxxxxx.dll</listItem>
        	<listItem>Add using xxxxxxxxx to your code</listItem>
        	<listItem>example code</listItem>
        	<listItem>connect</listItem>
        	<listItem>build and submit queries/Actions</listItem>
        	<listItem>or instantiate convenience objects and call their methods</listItem>
        	<listItem>logout</listItem>
        </list>
        <para>
        </para>
        <para>
        	Your program will first need to establish a connection to CORE by instantiating the 
        	<codeEntityReference>T:Rackspace.CTKAPI.Net.CTKAPI</codeEntityReference> class, passing a username and password to the constructor.  
        	By default, this will establish a connection to the CORE PRODUCTION environment.  A token will be generated and stored 
        	in the <codeEntityReference>P:Rackspace.CTKAPI.Net.CTKAPI.Token</codeEntityReference> property.  This token will be
        	automatically used for all subsequent Queries and Actions that are submitted.
        </para>
        <para>
        	You can, optionally, pass in the base URL for the DEV or STAGING CORE environment
        	(e.g. "https://staging.core.rackspace.com"), along with the username and password.
        	This URL will be used for all subsequent Queries and Actions that are submitted.
        </para>
        
        <para>
        	The connection object you just created will be used to submit Queries and Actions, and will also be passed to the 
        	<codeEntityReference>N:Rackspace.CTKAPI.Net.CTKObjects</codeEntityReference> class constructors.
        </para>
        
        <para>
        	Whether you choose to build Queries and Actions using the low-level classes or the CTK??????? classes, you will 
        	need to understand how to build <codeEntityReference>T:Rackspace.CTKAPI.Net.CTKWhereCondition</codeEntityReference>
        	objects and <codeEntityReference>T:Rackspace.CTKAPI.Net.CTKWhere</codeEntityReference> objects.  Refer to 
        	<externalLink>
			    <linkText>CORE CTKAPI</linkText>
			    <linkUri>https://ws.core.rackspace.com/ctkapi/doc/query/</linkUri>
			</externalLink>
			for more information on the underlying <legacyBold>Where<legacyBold> objects
        </para>
        
        <para>
        	The CTKWhere <codeEntityReference>P:Rackspace.CTKAPI.Net.CTKWhere.ClassName</codeEntityReference> must be set to a valid
        	CORE CTKAPI {Module.WhereTable_class}, like <literal>Computer.ComputerWhere</literal> or <literal>Account.AccountWhere</literal>.
        	
        </para>
        
        <para>
        	The CTKWhere <codeEntityReference>P:Rackspace.CTKAPI.Net.CTKWhere.Values</codeEntityReference> must be set to a 
        	<codeEntityReference>T:Rackspace.CTKAPI.Net.CTKWhereCondition</codeEntityReference> object.  A CTKWhereCondition is
        	created by passing <placeholder>operand1</placeholder>, <placeholder>operator</placeholder>, and <placeholder>operand2</placeholder>
        	to the constructor.
        </para>
        
        <para>
        	
        </para>
        
        <para>
        <codeEntityReference>T:Rackspace.CTKAPI.Net.CTKWhereCondition</codeEntityReference>
        </para>
      </content>
